/// Comparable — a total order derived from one required `<`.
///
/// A using class implements `<`; everything else — `>`, `<=`, `>=`,
/// `between:and:`, `min:`, `max:` — is derived and provided (ADR 0127 §1,
/// §9, decision 7). A class that already implements every selector below
/// conforms to `Comparable` whether or not it `uses:` it (ADR 0068).
///
/// `Number` deliberately does **not** `uses: Comparable`: it already
/// defines all four ordering operators and `between:and:` itself, and this
/// protocol's `min:`/`max:` would change `Number`'s existing tie semantics
/// (`3 max: 3.0` answers `3.0` today via `Number`'s own `max:`; this
/// protocol's `(self < other) ifTrue: [other] ifFalse: [self]` would answer
/// `3` instead). See `stdlib/src/number.bt` for `Number`'s own `min:`/`max:`.
///
/// ## Examples
/// ```beamtalk
/// (DateTime year: 2025 month: 1 day: 1) max: (DateTime year: 2026 month: 1 day: 1)
/// // => DateTime(2026-01-01T00:00:00Z)
/// (Duration seconds: 5) between: (Duration seconds: 1) and: (Duration seconds: 10)
/// // => true
/// ```
Protocol define: Comparable
  /// Required — strict ordering. The using class must implement this.
  < other :: Self -> Boolean

  // === Derived ordering ===

  /// True if the receiver sorts after `other`.
  > other :: Self -> Boolean => other < self

  /// True if the receiver sorts before or the same as `other`.
  <= other :: Self -> Boolean => (other < self) not

  /// True if the receiver sorts after or the same as `other`.
  >= other :: Self -> Boolean => (self < other) not

  /// True if the receiver lies in the closed interval `[min, max]`.
  between: min :: Self and: max :: Self -> Boolean =>
    (self >= min) and: [self <= max]

  /// Return the smaller of the receiver and `other`.
  min: other :: Self -> Self => (self < other) ifTrue: [self] ifFalse: [other]

  /// Return the larger of the receiver and `other`.
  max: other :: Self -> Self => (self < other) ifTrue: [other] ifFalse: [self]
